(:name "press"
 :text "Here is a short history of the printing press. Johannes Gutenberg, a goldsmith from Mainz, developed a system of movable metal type around 1440. Before that, books in Europe were copied by hand, usually by monks working in scriptoria, and a single Bible could take years to produce. Gutenberg combined several existing technologies: the screw press used for wine and olive oil, oil-based inks, and a hand mould for casting type quickly and precisely. His most famous work, the Gutenberg Bible, was printed in about 1455, and fewer than fifty copies survive today. Within fifty years, printing presses had spread to more than two hundred cities across Europe, and millions of books had been printed. The consequences were enormous. Literacy rose, scientific ideas travelled faster, and the Reformation spread its pamphlets far and wide. In short, the printing press"
